W: 1. I am a king’s daughter come straight from Cappoquin,
W: In search of Lord Gregory, may God I’ll find him.
W:
W: 2. The rain beats at my yellow locks, the dew wets me still,
W: The babe is cold in my arms, love, Lord Gregory let me in.
W:
W: 3. Lord Gregory is not here and he henceforth can’t be seen,
W: For he’s gone to bonny Scotland to bring home his new queen.
W:
W: 4. Leave now these windows and likewise this hall,
W: For it’s deep in the sea you will find your downfall.
W:
W: 5. Do you remember, love Gregory, as we sat at the wine?
W: We echanged rings and aye, the best was mine.
W:
W: 6. Yours was the purest gold and mine but false tin,
W: Yours it cost a guinea, love, but aye, ’twas false within.
W:
W: 7. Do you remember, love Gregory, that night in Cappoquin?
W: You stole away my maidenhead and sore against my will.
W:
W: 8. So open these windows, open and let me in,
W: The rain rains on my good clothing and the dew stands on my chin.
W:
W: 9. I have built a bonny boat all covered with pearl,
W: And at every needle tuck in it there hangs a silver bell.
W:
W: 10. But I’ll take down that mast of gold and set up a mast of tree,
W: For it does not suit a forsaken maid to sail so royally.
W:
W: 11. And I’ll leave now these windows and likewise this hall,
W: For it’s deep in the sea I will find my downfall.
%
